\section{Why 9 points, not the 5-point SSURGO/SOLUS input convention}{

The 5-point set (\code{c(0.05, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 0.95)}) is what SSURGO/SOLUS happen to provide as raw
\emph{input} percentile data - a real external constraint on the prior/likelihood side. The
\emph{posterior} has no such constraint, since this package computes it, and 5 points under-resolves
exactly the tail behavior risk/cost-assessment use cases need (exceedance probabilities,
asymmetric credible intervals). This set covers a 98\% interval (P01/P99, standard for tail-risk
work), a 90\% interval (P05/P95), and an 80\% interval (P10/P90), alongside the existing
median/IQR.
}

\section{Reliability note}{

For the closed-form analytic routes (\code{qnorm}/\code{qbeta}/\code{qgamma}), extra percentiles are free
and exact regardless of how extreme they are. For
\code{fuse_general_kde()}'s sample/grid-based route, percentiles are read directly off
\code{update_prior()}'s discretized \code{posterior_prob} (see that function's own "Grid-based
percentiles" section) - exact relative to \code{grid_resolution} and NOT resampling-noise-limited,
but still subject to how well the underlying KDE itself approximates the tails from a finite
\code{n_samples}-sized input resample; the extreme percentiles here (P01/P99) are inherently noisier
than the inner ones (P10-P90) for that reason, not because of resampling.

A caller needing even more extreme tail resolution (e.g. P0.1/P99.9) can pass its own
\code{posterior_probs} instead of relying on this default.
